# API Versioning ChangeLog

Our API versioning contains an `APIVER` and an `APIAGE`.
The `APIAGE` is the number of versions we're backward compatible with. (iow.  things got added
without breaking anything unaware of it.)

Future changes should be documented in here.

## Version 17:

* Add the volume types `vm-vol`, `vm-state` and `ct-vol`

  `parse_volname()` may return a more specific type than `images` for a guest volume: `vm-vol` for
  the disk of a VM, `vm-state` for a saved VM state (snapshot or hibernation) and `ct-vol` for a
  container volume. A plugin that cannot tell them apart can keep returning `images`, which is
  accepted wherever a VM volume type is expected.

  The in-tree plugins derive the type with `$class->guest_volume_vtype($format, $name)` of the base
  plugin, which other plugins with the same naming scheme can use too: a subvolume is a `ct-vol`,
  a volume named `vm-<VMID>-state-<NAME>` a `vm-state` and everything else a `vm-vol`. That
  includes raw container volumes, which are named like VM volumes.

  A plugin with an API version before 17 that inherits `parse_volname()` from an in-tree plugin
  keeps getting `images`, unless it calls that parser by class name, so use
  `$class->SUPER::parse_volname()`. When raising the API version, check where the plugin compares
  a volume type with `images`. `is_guest_vtype()` and `vtype_matches()` in `PVE::Storage::Common`
  cover the common cases.

  The content types `images` and `rootdir` allow all volume types of VMs and of containers. Check
  whether a storage allows a type with `PVE::Storage::Common::content_allows()` instead of looking
  it up in `$scfg->{content}`.

* Allow `vm-vol`, `vm-state` and `ct-vol` as content types

  A plugin can list them in the content types of its `plugindata()`, next to `images` and
  `rootdir`. A storage of its type can then be restricted to some of them, for example to VM
  states. `list_volumes()` is still only called with `images` and `rootdir` for guest volumes.

* Allow plugins to describe their properties for the web UI

  The web UI generates the dialog for adding and editing a storage of a third-party plugin from
  metadata that `GET /plugins/storage/plugin` returns for all plugins, optionally filtered by
  `origin` (`builtin` or `custom`), and `GET /plugins/storage/plugin/{type}` for one. It holds the
  origin, the supported and default content types and the schema of each option, with `optional`
  and `fixed` as in `options()` and the hints `sensitive`, `x-advanced` and `x-hidden`. This works
  for any supported API version. A plugin can improve its dialog with:

  - `title` in the schema of a property: the label of the field, which is otherwise derived from
    the name of the property.
  - `multiline` in the schema of a string property: a text field with several lines. It needs
    `libpve-common-perl` 9.2.10 and is only a hint, the section config still refuses values with
    line feeds. So list the property in `'sensitive-properties'` or encode the value in the
    plugin, for example in `check_config()`.
  - `'advanced-properties'` and `'hidden-properties'` in `plugindata()`, hashes like
    `'sensitive-properties'`: the former moves properties to the advanced section, the latter
    leaves them out of the dialog. A hidden required property is still shown when adding a
    storage. The fields for properties that all storages share, like `nodes`, `content` and the
    backup retention, only partly follow these two and `title`.
  - `meta => { 'display-name' => ... }` in `plugindata()`: the name shown for the storage type.

  A property is reported as `sensitive` if it is in `'sensitive-properties'`, in the fallback list
  of version 11 for a plugin without that hash, or if its schema sets `sensitive`. The web UI never
  shows such a value and leaves it unchanged if the field stays empty. Only
  `'sensitive-properties'` keeps a value out of `/etc/pve/storage.cfg` and passes it to the hooks
  of the plugin. With the schema keyword alone it is stored and returned by the API like any other
  property.

  A property in `'sensitive-properties'` has to be optional if listed in `options()`: its value is
  removed before the required options of a new storage are checked. A plugin that needs it can
  check in `on_add_hook()`, like the ESXi plugin does for its password.

* Allow plugins to isolate their properties

  With `'isolate-properties' => 1` in `plugindata()`, the properties a plugin declares belong to
  it alone: no other plugin can use them, and their names cannot clash with those of other
  plugins, including ones that in-tree plugins get later, only with the common properties below.
  Without it, a third-party plugin is not loaded if it declares a property that an in-tree
  plugin, or a third-party plugin loaded before it, declares too.

  It requires API version 17, so a plugin whose `api()` also reports older versions must only set
  it if `PVE::Storage::APIVER` is at least 17. Without it, the plugin has to list its properties
  in `options()` and use, not declare, a property that another plugin declares, like `server`.

  The properties an isolating plugin declares are its options without being listed in
  `options()`, and required unless their schema sets `optional`. Listing one there allows marking
  it `fixed`, and then also decides whether it is optional. What `options()` requires, the API
  requires as parameter, so a property that the plugin derives a value for has to be optional.

  Besides its own, such a plugin can only use the common properties, which the base plugin
  declares: `nodes`, `disable`, `shared`, `content`, `content-dirs`, `format`, `preallocation`,
  `prune-backups`, `max-protected-backups`, `path`, `create-base-path`, `create-subdirs`,
  `bwlimit`, `base`, `snapshot-as-volume-chain` and the deprecated `mkdir`. Code outside of the
  plugins reads these from any storage, for example `path` to tell whether its volumes are files,
  so the plugin must not declare one of them itself, nor the API parameters `delete` and
  `digest`. The properties of in-tree plugins, like `server`, `username` or `port`, are not
  available. The plugin declares the ones it needs itself and can use the standard option
  `pve-storage-port` and the registered formats, like `pve-storage-server`, for them. A plugin
  that breaks one of these rules, or sets `'expose-properties'`, is not loaded.

  A common property is only added with a new API version, which is announced here. A plugin that
  declares a property of that name is then not loaded until it renames it, so prefer names that
  are specific to the plugin, for example prefixed with its type.

  As soon as one such plugin is installed, the API checks the parameters for adding and updating a
  storage against the options of its type alone, for all storage types. Only the sensitive
  properties of a plugin that does not isolate its own are still accepted without being listed in
  its `options()`, by their own names: an alias that only another plugin declares for one is not.

  Set 'sensitive-properties' in an isolating plugin, even if empty. Without it, the fallback list of
  version 11 also applies to the plugin's own properties of these names.

* Pass the guest identity to backup providers

  The info hash of `backup_vm()`, `backup_container_prepare()` and `backup_container()` has the
  `guest-uid` and the registration time `guest-rtime` of the guest, and the `cluster-id`. A key is
  left out if its value is not known. The guest config of the call has the same values in its
  `meta` property. Whether the keys are passed depends on the versions of qemu-server and
  pve-container, not on the API version, see `PVE::BackupProvider::Plugin::Base`.

  A restore takes the identity from the `meta` property of the config that
  `archive_get_guest_config()` returns, so return that config unchanged. A restore over a guest with
  another `uid` is refused unless `allow-identity-mismatch` is set.

* Run cleanup workers of `free_image()` without the caller's locks

  `free_image()` can return a code reference, which `vdisk_free()` runs in a forked `imgdel` task
  after releasing the storage lock. The caller may still hold other locks at that point, like the
  config or migration lock of the guest. The task now closes the inherited handles of locks taken
  with `PVE::Tools::lock_file_full()`, so it no longer keeps the guest locked until the cleanup is
  done. A worker therefore has to take the locks it needs itself, like the storage lock with
  `cluster_lock_storage()`, and must not rely on the guest staying locked while it runs. This
  depends on the version of pve-storage, not on the API version.

* Activate a new storage after releasing the configuration lock

  Adding a storage calls `activate_storage()` once its section is written and the lock on the
  storage configuration is released, so that waiting for a network does not block other changes to
  the configuration. As another activation can run at the same time, a failed one is retried once.
  If it still fails, the section is removed again, unless it changed or became the base of another
  storage meanwhile. Other nodes can activate the storage meanwhile and keep what they connected or
  mounted. The base storage and volume of an LVM storage on an iSCSI or NVMe over Fabrics base are
  activated before the lock is taken, so that `on_add_hook()` usually finds them active. This
  depends on the version of pve-storage, not on the API version.

## Version 16:

* Add `volname_for_format()`, `get_parsed_format()`, `volname_with_format()`, `is_valid_format()` and
  `get_format_extension()` plugin methods together with the `FORMAT_EXTENSION` constant

  These are called via the plugin class when allocating or renaming a volume, to check that a
  caller-supplied name agrees with the requested format and to adapt an extension-less name. The
  base implementations cover directory-like plugins, where every format carries an extension, and
  the in-tree block-based plugins override `get_parsed_format()` and `FORMAT_EXTENSION` to match
  their naming scheme. Plugins that implement `alloc_image()` and `rename_volume()` without calling
  the base implementation are not affected.

## Version 15:

* Add new `$snapname` parameter to the `volume_resize()` plugin method

  If specified, the snapshot is the target of the resize operation. This is currently only used in
  combination with the `snapshot-as-volume-chain` configuration option before a commit operation.
  Plugins that do not support the `snapshot-as-volume-chain` configuration option may either die
  when `$snapname` is set or must implement resizing snapshots.

* Add `virtual-size` to the information returned by the `volume_snapshot_info()` plugin method

  This is the virtual size of the volume at the time the snapshot was taken. This information is
  required for storages with the `snapshot-as-volume-chain` configuration option, to make snapshot
  removal work correctly in combination with resizing images.

* Properly document `volume_snapshot_info()` plugin method

  If you already implement this method, check that the returned information is in line with the
  documentation.

## Version 14:

* Introduce `get_identity()` plugin method

  Plugins can implement this method to return a unique identifier for the underlying storage
  instance. The format and semantics of the returned identifier are up to the concrete plugin,
  for example a UUID or a server-provided instance ID that identifies the backend independently
  of how the storage is configured in `/etc/pve/storage.cfg`.

  The base implementation dies, so plugins for which the concept of a stable backend identity
  does not apply may leave the method unimplemented. Implement this method when the storage
  backend exposes an identity that consumers may want to correlate across storage definitions.

## Version 13:

* Add new parameter $hints to the `activate_volume()` and `map_volume()` plugin methods

  Old signature of `activate_volume()`:

    sub($plugin, $storeid, $scfg, $volname, $snapname, $cache)

  New signature of `activate_volume()`:

    sub($plugin, $storeid, $scfg, $volname, $snapname, $cache, $hints)

  Old signature of `map_volume()`:

    sub($plugin, $storeid, $scfg, $volname, $snapname)

  New signature of `map_volume()`:

    sub($plugin, $storeid, $scfg, $volname, $snapname, $hints)

  Hints are a mechanism that allows the upper layers to optionally pass down well-defined
  information to the storage plugins on volume activation/mapping. The storage plugin can make
  adjustments to its volume activation/mapping based on the hints (we call this "applying" the
  hint).

  If the new parameter $hints is defined, it is a hash reference that adheres to the schema
  `PVE::Storage::Plugin::$hints_format` (verified by caller). The supported hints are specified in
  `PVE::Storage::Plugin::$hints_properties` and may be extended in the future. It is not guaranteed
  that the volume is inactive when `activate_volume()` or `map_volume()` are called, and it is not
  guaranteed that hints are passed on every storage activation. Hints may be passed at additional
  storage activation call sites in the future without an API version bump.

  It can happen a volume is already active but applying the hint would require unmapping the volume
  and mapping it again with the hint applied. To cover such cases, the Boolean hint
  'plugin-may-deactivate-volume' denotes whether unmapping the volume is currently safe. Only if
  this hint is true, the plugin may deactivate the volume and map it again with the hint applied.

  Hints may be added in the future without an API version bump. Plugins may call
  `PVE::Storage::Plugin::is_hint_supported()` to check whether a specific hint is supported.

  Plugins that do not require hints can safely ignore the additional parameter.

* Introduce `on_update_hook_full()` plugin method

  The original `on_update_hook()` plugin method was limited, because only the updated properties and
  values would be passed in. The new `on_update_hook_full()` plugin method also receives the current
  storage configuration and the list of which properties are to be deleted. This allows detecting
  and reacting to all changes and knowing how values changed. See also bug #6669 [0] for the initial
  motiviation. If a plugin implements `on_update_hook_full()`, that method will be called rather
  than the `on_update_hook()` method.

  [0]: https://bugzilla.proxmox.com/show_bug.cgi?id=6669

## Version 12:

* Introduce `qemu_blockdev_options()` plugin method

  Proxmox VE will switch to the more modern QEMU command line option `-blockdev` replacing `-drive`.
  With `-drive`, it was enough to specify a path, where special protocol paths like `iscsi://` were
  also supported. With `-blockdev`, the data is more structured, a driver needs to be specified
  alongside the path to an image and each driver supports driver-specific options. Most storage
  plugins should be fine using driver `host_device` in case of a block device and `file` in case of
  a file and no special options. See the default implemenation of the base plugin for guidance, also
  if the plugin uses protocol paths. Implement this method for Proxmox VE 9.

  See `$allowed_qemu_blockdev_options` in `PVE/Storage.pm` for currently allowed drivers and option.
  Feel free to request allowing more drivers or options on the pve-devel mailing list based on your
  needs.

* Introduce `rename_snapshot()` plugin method

  This method allow to rename a vm disk snapshot name to a different snapshot name.

* Introduce `volume_qemu_snapshot_method()` plugin method

  This method declares how snapshots should be handled for *running* VMs.

  This should return one of the following:
    'qemu':
      Qemu must perform the snapshot. The storage plugin does nothing.
    'storage':
      The storage plugin *transparently* performs the snapshot and the running VM does not need to
      do anything.
    'mixed': 
      For taking a snapshot: The storage performs an offline snapshot and qemu then has to reopen
      the volume.
      For removing a snapshot: One of 2 things will happen (both must be supported):
      a) Qemu will "unhook" the snapshot by moving its data into the child snapshot, and then call
         `volume_snapshot_delete` with `running` set, in which case the storage should delete only
         the snapshot without touching the surrounding snapshots.
      b) Qemu will "commit" the child snapshot to the one which is being removed, then call
         `volume_snapshot_delete()` on the child snapshot, then call `rename_snapshot()` to move the
         merged snapshot into place.
      NOTE: Storages must support using "current" as a special name in `rename_snapshot()` to
      cheaply convert a snapshot into the current disk state and back.

* Introduce `get_formats()` plugin method

  Get information about the supported formats and default format according to the current storage
  configuration. The default implemenation is backwards-compatible with previous behavior and looks
  at the definition given in the plugin data, as well as the `format` storage configuration option,
  which can override the default format. Must be implemented when the supported formats or default
  format depend on the storage configuration.

##  Version 11:

* Allow declaring storage features via plugin data

  A new `storage_has_feature()` helper function was added that checks a storage plugin's features.
  Plugins can indicate support for certain features in their `plugindata`. The first such feature is
  `backup-provider`, see below for more details. To declare support for this feature, return
  `features => { 'backup-provider' => 1 }` as part of the plugin data.

* Introduce `new_backup_provider()` plugin method

  Proxmox VE now supports a `Backup Provider API` that can be used to implement custom backup
  solutions tightly integrated in the Proxmox VE stack. See the `PVE::BackupProvider::Plugin::Base`
  module for detailed documentation. A backup provider also needs to implement an associated storage
  plugin for user-facing integration in Proxmox VE. Such a plugin needds to opt-in to the
  `backup-provider` feature (see above) and implement the new_backup_provider() method, returning a
  blessed reference to the backup provider class. The rest of the plugin methods, e.g. listing
  content, providing usage information, etc., follow the same API as usual.

* Allow declaring sensitive properties via plugin data

  A new `sensitive_properties()` helper function was added to get the list of sensitive properties
  a plugin uses via the plugin's `plugindata`. The sensitive properties are passed separately from
  other properties to the `on_add_hook()` and `on_update_hook()` methods and should not be written
  to the storage configuration file directly, but stored in the more restricted
  `/etc/pve/priv/storage` directory on the Proxmox Cluster File System. For example, to declare that
  a `ssh-private-key` property used by the plugin is sensitive, return
  `'sensitive-properties' => { 'ssh-private-key' => 1 }` as part of the plugin data. The list of
  sensitive properties was hard-coded previously, as `encryption-key`, `keyring`, `master-pubkey`,
  `password`. For backwards compatibility, this list is still used if a plugin doesn't declare its
  own sensitive properties.

##  Version 10:

* a new `rename_volume` method has been added

  Storage plugins with rename support need to enable
  the `rename` feature flag; e.g. in the `volume_has_feature` method.

* Replace `volume_snapshot_list` with `volume_snapshot_info`:

  `volume_snapshot_list` was used exclusively by replication and currently, replication is only
  allowed for the storage type `zfspool`. Thus, no external plugins should be affected by this
  change and `APIAGE` is *not* reset.

  `volume_snapshot_info` returns a hash with snapshot names as keys and `id` and `timestamp` data
  for each snapshot, rather than just an array of snaphsot names like `volume_snapshot_list` did.

* Add `blockers` parameter to `volume_rollback_is_possible`:

  The parameter *can* be used to return a list of snapshots that is currently preventing rollback.

* Replace get/update_volume_notes with generic get/update_volume_attribute

  falling back to the old implementation for notes until we reset APIAGE. the
  new method optionally also supports querying/setting a protected flag.

##  Version 9: (AGE resets to 0):

* volume_import_formats gets a new parameter *inserted*:

  Old signature:
      sub($plugin, $scfg, $storeid, $volname, $base_snapshot, $with_snapshots)
  New signature:
      sub($plugin, $scfg, $storeid, $volname, $snapshot, $base_snapshot, $with_snapshots)

  This is now the same as `volume_export_formats`.

  The same goes for calls to `PVE::Storage::volume_import_formats`, which now
  takes a `$snapshot` parameter in the same place.

* $with_snapshots *may* now be an array reference containing an ordered list of
  snapshots, but *may* also just be a boolean, and the contained list *may* be
  ignored, so it can still be treated as a boolean.

